00:01:53.321 --> 00:01:55.824
Please answer ASAP.

00:01:57.700 --> 00:01:58.993
This whole process is

00:01:58.993 --> 00:02:02.789
called CA construction
contract administration.

00:02:03.039 --> 00:02:06.459
This is the phase after the design phase,

00:02:06.835 --> 00:02:11.256
after you've created all the construction
drawings and written all your specs.

00:02:11.548 --> 00:02:13.925
After you've selected your contractor.

00:02:14.175 --> 00:02:17.470
And now you're getting ready
to build your design.

00:02:17.887 --> 00:02:21.307
Typically, the architect is responsible
for administering

00:02:21.307 --> 00:02:24.561
the contract between the client
and the contractor.

00:02:24.602 --> 00:02:27.814
You have to make sure
that the proper products are ordered

00:02:28.064 --> 00:02:31.484
and if there's any questions,
they get answered quickly.

00:02:31.860 --> 00:02:37.365
And basically making sure that the design
is being built to your design intent.

00:02:37.448 --> 00:02:39.868
We do everything from reviewing

00:02:39.868 --> 00:02:42.912
shop drawings or answering questions
from the contractors.

00:02:43.663 --> 00:02:44.873
We do site reviews.

00:02:44.873 --> 00:02:49.794
We certify payments, and if the contractor
claims for any extras,

00:02:49.794 --> 00:02:55.425
we administer that to make sure that it's
fair and warranted for this project.

00:02:55.508 --> 00:02:58.595
Is a renovation of an existing building.

00:02:58.636 --> 00:03:02.515
So a lot of things did come up
that we didn't really expect.

00:03:02.557 --> 00:03:05.643
For example,
we're demolishing these existing washrooms

00:03:05.685 --> 00:03:08.855
to turn them into universal,
gender neutral washrooms.

00:03:08.855 --> 00:03:12.233
But when these scandals slide,
to start caring for the toilets,

00:03:12.609 --> 00:03:17.780
they found electrical conduits in the slab
that weren't on the original drawings.

00:03:18.198 --> 00:03:23.453
Now, a really big part of an architect's
job is coordinating between all the

00:03:23.453 --> 00:03:27.332
different parties involved in construction
and with your consultants.

00:03:27.874 --> 00:03:31.711
So first
all go to our electrical consultant

00:03:31.711 --> 00:03:34.714
and I'll ask him,
is it okay if we core into this?

00:03:34.797 --> 00:03:39.177
And in this case, he was like, Yeah, it's
potentially an important conduit,

00:03:39.636 --> 00:03:44.140
but it will be impossible
to know what it leads to

00:03:44.307 --> 00:03:49.562
unless we actually trace the conduit
in the slab back to the source.

00:03:49.687 --> 00:03:52.690
And of course, in a perfect world,

00:03:52.690 --> 00:03:57.737
with an infinite amount of time and money,
we would probably do that.

00:03:57.946 --> 00:04:01.532
co the library,
they need to open at a certain time

00:04:01.532 --> 00:04:03.076
so that they can serve the public.

00:04:03.076 --> 00:04:05.870
So then you have a chat
with the contractor.

00:04:05.870 --> 00:04:07.121
Is this feasible?

00:04:07.121 --> 00:04:09.540
Realistically, how long do you think
this is going to take?

00:04:09.666 --> 00:04:14.003
And so after talking to them,
you might be like, okay, maybe a better

00:04:14.003 --> 00:04:18.716
solution is just to go with walking
toilets instead of floor mounted.

00:04:18.758 --> 00:04:23.513
But if we do that, we're going to need
a much bigger plumbing wall here.

00:04:23.554 --> 00:04:27.225
And now it's an architectural issue.

00:04:27.308 --> 00:04:30.770
These stalls are getting really,
really tight.

00:04:31.062 --> 00:04:34.065
So you could move this wall out

00:04:34.148 --> 00:04:37.360
a little bit, which is going to eat
into the whole atrium.

00:04:37.360 --> 00:04:40.822
And so you go and ask the library,
what do you think about this?

00:04:41.114 --> 00:04:44.325
And in this case, the answer was no,

00:04:44.325 --> 00:04:47.412
because they would be losing
an entire bookshelf.

00:04:47.996 --> 00:04:53.835
And so now you go to your last resort,
which is to move this small six

00:04:53.835 --> 00:04:55.253
inches down.

00:04:55.420 --> 00:04:58.506
So you're not going to get your five foot
turning radius here.

00:04:58.506 --> 00:05:02.677
But, you know, by code,
that's actually okay

00:05:02.719 --> 00:05:07.390
because this over here
is the only accessible stall.

00:05:07.598 --> 00:05:10.685
So anyways, your job as an architect

00:05:10.935 --> 00:05:15.023
is to talk to all of the parties
involved with the building code

00:05:15.023 --> 00:05:18.151
and accessibility in mind
and try to come up

00:05:18.151 --> 00:05:22.155
with the best possible solution
after everything's been considered.

00:05:22.447 --> 00:05:24.032
When you're in construction.

00:05:24.032 --> 00:05:27.910
Everything is going to seem super,
super, super urgent.

00:05:28.536 --> 00:05:33.499
But a part of being an architect
and doing C is learning

00:05:33.499 --> 00:05:38.588
to separate the really urgent ones
from the ones that can kind of wait.

00:05:40.089 --> 00:05:43.134
Ari, let's talk about something
a little bit more exciting.

00:05:43.468 --> 00:05:47.347
Materials, these are the options
for the concrete finish.

00:05:47.638 --> 00:05:49.724
This is going to go in
throughout the building.

00:05:50.183 --> 00:05:53.644
And so what we're doing
is we're specifying two different

00:05:53.644 --> 00:05:56.606
aggregate sizes
that are going to go side by side

00:05:57.065 --> 00:06:01.611
and there's going to be aluminum
reveal in between them.

00:06:02.028 --> 00:06:04.072
So they're going to have
the exact same color,

00:06:04.655 --> 00:06:07.241
but they're going to have different
aggregate sizes.

00:06:07.283 --> 00:06:13.664
The other thing to note here is we have
existing concrete columns in the building,

00:06:13.664 --> 00:06:18.252
and so we have to make sure
that this concrete overlay meant

00:06:18.252 --> 00:06:22.673
that we're putting in here matches
or at least works

00:06:22.840 --> 00:06:25.843
with the existing concrete columns,

00:06:26.219 --> 00:06:29.347
which are slightly on the brown side.

00:06:29.472 --> 00:06:35.019
So I'm kind of leaning away from
these cooler tones right now.

00:06:36.396 --> 00:06:39.857
Option one has a 1.2 to 2 mm.

00:06:39.857 --> 00:06:43.319
Aggregate and the spacing between
the aggregates are tighter.

00:06:43.569 --> 00:06:44.862
I think this is probably the best

00:06:44.862 --> 00:06:48.491
for hiding imperfections
or scratches between two houses.

00:06:48.783 --> 00:06:51.160
There's going to be a lot of other
like four. Mm.

00:06:51.160 --> 00:06:55.331
Option three has a zero point
to one, 3 to 1 point

00:06:55.331 --> 00:06:58.292
or two much better
than the other aggregate size.

00:06:59.710 --> 00:07:01.921
I decided to make a quick visit to psych

